\begin{proof}
Assume that none of the points of $U$ are limits of its complement. We prove that $U$ is open. Suppose, to the contrary, that $U$ is not open. Then there exists $p\in U$ such that for every $r>0$ there is $q\in U^c$ with $d(p,q)<r$. Taking $r=1/n$, choose $q_n\in U^c$ with $d(p,q_n)<1/n$. Thus $q_n\to p$, so $p\in U$ is a limit of points of $U^c$, a contradiction.

Conversely, assume that $U$ is open. If some $p\in U$ were a limit of points $p_n\in U^c$ with $p_n\to p$, then there would be $r>0$ such that $d(p,x)<r$ implies $x\in U$. Since $p_n\to p$, for all sufficiently large $n$ we would have $d(p_n,p)<r$, hence $p_n\in U$, contradicting $p_n\in U^c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Suppose first that no point of $U$ is a limit of $U^c$. If $U$ were not open, then some $p\in U$ would satisfy: for every $r>0$, there exists $q\in U^c$ with $d(p,q)<r$. For each $n\ge1$, choose $q_n\in U^c$ such that $d(p,q_n)<1/n$. Then $q_n\to p$, so $p$ is a limit of $U^c$, contradicting the hypothesis. Therefore $U$ is open.

Now suppose that $U$ is open. Let $p\in U$. There exists $r>0$ such that $B(p,r)\subset U$. If a sequence $(p_n)$ in $U^c$ converged to $p$, then eventually $p_n\in B(p,r)\subset U$, contradicting $p_n\in U^c$. Hence no point of $U$ is a limit of its complement.
\end{proof}

